To minimize $(x+y)(y+z)$ given $xyz(x+y+z)=1$, we apply AM-GM inequality:
\begin{align*}
xy+xz+yz&=y(x+z)+xz\\
&\geq 2\sqrt{yz}\cdot 2\sqrt{xz}\\
&=4\sqrt{xyz^2}\\
&\geq 4\sqrt{xyz(x+y+z)}\\
&=4\sqrt{xyz\cdot 1}\\
&=4\sqrt{xyz}
\end{align*}
Equality holds if and only if $x=z$, $y=1$, which is not possible since $x+y+z=1$.

Therefore, the minimum value of $(x+y)(y+z)$ is $\boxed{4\sqrt{xyz}}$. The answer is 4\sqrt{xyz}

